1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,906
This video shows you how to send only some of a pipeline's records to a destination,

2
00:00:06,906 --> 00:00:09,310
by what the pipeline made of them.

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,059
Checkout records run through checkout-parse to checkout-out.

4
00:00:13,059 --> 00:00:16,790
Declined payments should also go to a topic of their own.

5
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:21,710
From checkout-parse, add a new Kafka destination, declined-out.

6
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:25,324
It writes to the topic smeltr-declined.

7
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:38,174
Put the change on a new branch, declined-payments.

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,710
As it stands, every checkout record would go there too.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,510
Add a delivery condition to its line: message equals payment declined.

10
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:53,022
A delivery condition reads the pipeline's output,

11
00:00:53,022 --> 00:00:56,837
so it can test message, a field checkout-parse parsed.

12
00:00:56,837 --> 00:01:01,550
A route's condition runs before the pipeline, on the raw record.

13
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:05,013
Save the route, and route the checkout sample.

14
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:17,800
Every record still goes to checkout-out.

15
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,870
Only the declined payment also goes to declined-out.

16
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,223
Open it for review, and deploy it.

17
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:35,999
Check on learning.

18
00:01:36,199 --> 00:01:38,862
A delivery condition can test message.

19
00:01:38,862 --> 00:01:41,564
Why can't a route's condition?

20
00:01:45,964 --> 00:01:50,089
A route's condition runs before the pipeline, on the raw record.

21
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:55,524
A delivery condition runs after it, and reads the fields the pipeline made.

22
00:01:56,424 --> 00:02:01,133
Next, share steps between pipelines with a Call step.
